# Lore, part 8 (0.1.25): fuller entries for the peoples that were still thin.
LORE = {
'shan': ("The Shan are a Tai people of the hills and valleys of eastern Burma; their thirty-odd states were ruled by saopha princes under Burmese kings and the British, and the princes gave up their powers in 1959, after which many Shan took up arms for autonomy.",
         "They hold the Poy Sang Long ordination, where boys are dressed as princes before becoming novices, eat sticky rice, Shan noodles and fermented soybean, grow tea on the hills, and celebrate their new year with drums and long-necked lanterns."),
'tay_nung': ("The Tay and Nung are Tai peoples of the northern mountains of Vietnam, rice farmers of the valleys who lived beside the Chinese border for centuries and fought in the resistance against France, which began in their hills at Viet Bac.",
             "They play the dan tinh, a long-necked gourd lute, to accompany then songs that are both prayer and poetry, wear indigo-dyed clothes, live in stilt houses, and hold the Long Tong festival of going down to the fields."),
'kachin': ("The Kachin, Jingpo and their kin live in the hills of northern Burma and Yunnan; Baptist missionaries made most of them Christian, and the Kachin Independence Army has fought for autonomy since 1961, in a land rich with jade and amber.",
           "At the Manau festival thousands dance in winding lines around tall painted posts that tell their origin stories, led by men in hornbill headdresses; they carry long swords and silver-studded bags, and their jade mines at Hpakant are the richest on Earth."),
'breton': ("The Bretons came from Britain in the fifth and sixth centuries, fleeing the Anglo-Saxons and naming their new land after the old; Brittany was an independent duchy until it joined France in 1532, and its Celtic language survived in the west.",
           "They keep pardons, pilgrimages to local saints with banners and lace coiffes, dance at the fest-noz to the bombard and biniou pipes, eat crepes and galettes with cider, and gather at the great Lorient festival of the Celtic nations."),
'kpelle': ("The Kpelle are the largest people of Liberia and live also in Guinea; upland rice farmers of the forest, they kept their Poro and Sande societies through the founding of Liberia by freed American slaves and the wars of the 1990s.",
           "They play the triangle-frame zither and the slit drum, tell meni-pele, stories told with music where the audience joins in, and their young people pass through bush schools where they learn the law and crafts of the community."),
'nepali': ("The Nepalis, Khas-speaking hill people of Gorkha and the valleys, were united by Prithvi Narayan Shah in 1768 into a kingdom that was never colonised; its Gurkha soldiers became famous, and Nepal became a republic in 2008.",
           "They celebrate Dashain with blessings of red tika and kites in the autumn sky, Tihar with lamps and garlands even for dogs and crows, eat dal bhat twice a day and momo dumplings, and play the madal drum and sarangi in the villages."),
'ainu': ("The Ainu are the indigenous people of Hokkaido, Sakhalin and the Kuril islands, hunters, fishers and traders; Japan annexed Hokkaido in 1869 and banned their customs, and Japan recognised them as an indigenous people only in 2019.",
         "They hold the iyomante, the sending-off of the bear's spirit, carve wood with flowing patterns, weave attus cloth from elm bark, women once tattooed their lips, and they tell yukar, long sung epics of gods and heroes."),
'greenlander': ("The New Greenlanders are a people of Inuit, Danish and newcomer families who filled the thawing coasts of Greenland in the twenty-second century, after the island's home rule became independence.",
                "They fish and mine, farm potatoes and sheep in the sheltered southern fjords, keep the Inuit drum dance and kaffemik open-house gatherings, and celebrate the return of the sun after the winter darkness."),
'chuvash': ("The Chuvash are the only surviving speakers of the Oghur branch of Turkic, heirs of Volga Bulgaria, a medieval trading state on the Volga; they became Orthodox Christians under Russian rule while keeping old pagan customs.",
            "Their women wear headdresses covered in silver coins and dresses embroidered in red, they celebrate Akatuy, the feast of the plough, and Surkhuri at midwinter, and brew beer for every feast, which they call the drink of the gods."),
'occitan': ("Occitania, the south of France, was the land of the troubadours and of the Cathar heresy, crushed by the Albigensian crusade in the thirteenth century; its language, the langue d'oc, gave the region of Languedoc its name.",
            "They cook cassoulet in Toulouse and bouillabaisse in Marseille, play rugby with passion, sing in Occitan at summer festivals, and remember the troubadours, who invented the poetry of courtly love."),
'rakhine': ("The Rakhine had their own kingdom of Mrauk U on the Bay of Bengal, a trading power that used Muslim titles beside Buddhist ones and fought the Portuguese, until the Burmese conquest of 1784 carried off its great Mahamuni image.",
            "They celebrate the Thingyan water festival with boat races, are known for spicy seafood and Rakhine noodles, and the ruined temples of Mrauk U, stone fortresses of Buddhism, are their pride."),
'timorese': ("The peoples of Timor, Flores and the Moluccas are Austronesian and Papuan; the Moluccas were the spice islands Europeans fought over, and East Timor won its independence in 2002 after a long occupation by Indonesia.",
             "They weave tais and ikat cloth with ancestral patterns, build sacred houses, uma lulik, with high roofs, keep Catholic faith beside ancestral traditions, and the people of Flores hunt whales from rowing boats at Lamalera."),
'toraja': ("The Toraja of the mountains of Sulawesi kept their own religion, Aluk To Dolo, the way of the ancestors, long after the coasts became Muslim; Dutch missionaries later made many Christian.",
           "They hold great funerals that last days, with buffalo sacrifices and feasts for the whole village, keep their dead in cliff graves guarded by tau-tau wooden effigies, and build tongkonan houses with roofs like boats."),
'temne_mende': ("The Temne and Mende are the largest peoples of Sierra Leone; the Mende rose in the Hut Tax War of 1898 against the British, and both peoples lived through the civil war of the 1990s.",
                "The Sande society of women carves sowei helmet masks, the only masks in Africa worn by women, and the Poro society initiates young men; they eat cassava leaves and rice, and play the segbureh rattle."),
'batak': ("The Batak of northern Sumatra lived around Lake Toba, the largest volcanic lake on Earth, with their own script, priests and clans; German missionaries made many of them Protestant in the nineteenth century.",
          "They build houses with soaring saddle roofs, weave ulos cloth given at every life event, sing in famous church choirs, play the gondang drums, and eat saksang and arsik fish."),
'dai': ("The Dai of Xishuangbanna are a Tai people whose kingdom of Sipsongpanna, the twelve thousand rice fields, was ruled by its own princes until 1950.",
        "They are Theravada Buddhists who celebrate the water-splashing festival, dance the peacock dance, live in bamboo stilt houses, and eat sour and spicy food with sticky rice."),
'metis': ("The Metis grew from French and Scottish fur traders and Cree and Ojibwe women in the Red River valley; under Louis Riel they resisted Canada in 1869 and 1885, and they are recognised as one of Canada's aboriginal peoples.",
          "They dance the Red River jig to fiddle music, wear the colourful arrow sash, cook bannock and stew, and some speak Michif, a mix of French nouns and Cree verbs."),
'toubou': ("The Toubou are Saharan nomads of the Tibesti and Ennedi mountains of Chad, Niger and Libya, famously tough and independent, who resisted the French and every central government.",
           "They herd camels and goats, live in palm-mat tents, are known for endurance in the desert, and settle disputes by their own law and the payment of blood money."),
'tujia': ("The Tujia of the Wuling mountains were ruled by hereditary native chieftains from the Song to the Qing, when the empire replaced them with officials in the eighteenth century.",
          "They weave xilankapu brocade, dance the hand-waving dance in the first month of the year, and brides sing crying songs for days before their weddings."),
'albanian': ("The Albanians may descend from the ancient Illyrians; their hero Skanderbeg fought the Ottomans in the fifteenth century, and Albania declared independence in 1912; Albanians live also in Kosovo and North Macedonia.",
             "They keep besa, the word of honour that once protected Jews in the Second World War, sing iso-polyphony, celebrate Flag Day on 28 November, and are famously hospitable."),
'galician': ("Galicia was a Celtic and Suevi land, then a kingdom of its own in the Middle Ages; the tomb of Saint James at Santiago de Compostela made it the end of Europe's greatest pilgrimage road.",
             "They play the gaita bagpipe, eat octopus, empanada and shellfish, drink Albarino wine, and speak Galician, close to Portuguese; their emigrants filled Buenos Aires and Havana."),
'otomi_totonac': ("The Otomi and Totonac are ancient peoples of central Mexico; the Totonacs of Cempoala allied with Cortes against the Aztecs, and the Otomi lived in the valleys around Mexico City.",
                  "The Totonacs perform the voladores ritual, four men flying around a high pole tied by ropes, and grow vanilla; the Otomi embroider bright cloths and make paper from bark."),
'tuvan': ("The Tuvans are a Turkic people of the upper Yenisei; Tuva was an independent republic between 1921 and 1944 before joining the Soviet Union.",
          "They are masters of throat singing, khoomei, wrestle in the khuresh, herd yaks, horses and reindeer, and keep shamanism and Buddhism side by side."),
'delta_diaspora': ("The Delta diaspora are the people of the drowned Nile, Ganges and Mekong deltas, scattered when the sea rose in the twenty-second century and resettled in new cities inland.",
                   "They keep memories of home in songs, food and festivals of the old rivers, and they build tight-knit neighbourhoods with their own markets and temples in their new cities."),
'slovene': ("The Slovenes lived for a thousand years under Habsburg rule and kept their language through Protestant writers and the poet Preseren; Slovenia became independent in 1991.",
            "They are famous beekeepers with painted hive fronts, bake potica, ski and climb Triglav, and gather in vineyards for wine and song."),
'avar': ("The Avars and Dagestani peoples live in the mountains of Dagestan, the land of mountains and languages, where Imam Shamil led the resistance against Russia for twenty-five years.",
         "They are famous wrestlers, make carpets, silver and pottery in craft villages like Kubachi, and live in stone villages perched on cliffs."),
'ryukyuan': ("The Ryukyu Kingdom traded between China, Japan and Southeast Asia from Shuri castle until Japan annexed it in 1879; the Battle of Okinawa in 1945 killed a quarter of the people.",
             "They play the sanshin, a snakeskin lute, invented karate, eat goya and pork dishes, keep tomb festivals with the ancestors, and are famous for long lives."),
'yolngu': ("The Yolngu of north-eastern Arnhem Land traded with Makassar sailors for centuries before the British came; their bark petitions of 1963 began the land rights movement in Australia.",
           "They play the yidaki, the didgeridoo, paint bark with sacred clan designs, sing manikay song cycles, and hold the Garma festival of culture every year."),
'acehnese': ("The Sultanate of Aceh was a powerful trading state and centre of Islamic learning in the seventeenth century; it fought the Dutch for thirty years and later Indonesia, until peace in 2005.",
             "They dance the Saman, a seated dance of clapping and singing, drink strong Aceh coffee, and keep sharia under special autonomy."),
'raramuri': ("The Raramuri live in the Copper Canyon of northern Mexico; they retreated to the mountains from Spanish missions and silver mines.",
             "They are famous long-distance runners in sandals, kicking a wooden ball for days in races, brew tesguino maize beer, and hold Easter ceremonies mixing Catholic and older rites."),
'ndebele': ("The Ndebele of Zimbabwe were founded by Mzilikazi, a general of Shaka who broke away and moved north; their kingdom under Lobengula fell to Rhodes's company in 1893.",
            "The South African Ndebele are famous for their painted houses, and their women paint geometric murals and wear beaded neck rings and blankets."),
'tlingit_yupik': ("The Tlingit, Yup'ik and other Alaska Natives have lived in Alaska for millennia; the Tlingit fought the Russians at Sitka in 1802 and 1804.",
                  "They carve totem poles, hold potlatches, weave Chilkat blankets, and the Yup'ik dance with masks and hunt seals."),
'guarani_ind': ("The Guarani peoples once lived across much of South America; the Jesuit missions organised them, and they fought the Guarani War of 1756 against Spain and Portugal.",
                "They seek the Land Without Evil in their prophetic religion, pray and dance in opy houses, speak Guarani, and grow manioc and maize in the forest."),
'guyanese': ("The Guyanese and Surinamese are peoples of Indian, African, Javanese, Chinese, Amerindian and European roots, made by British and Dutch plantations on the Wild Coast.",
             "They eat pepperpot and roti, play cricket, and celebrate Diwali, Phagwah, Eid, Christmas and Mashramani in the same streets."),
'kyrgyz': ("The Kyrgyz are mountain nomads whose epic Manas is one of the longest in the world; they came to the Tian Shan from Siberia centuries ago and became part of Russia in the nineteenth century.",
           "They live in yurts in summer pastures, play kok-boru, a horse game with a goat carcass, hunt with golden eagles, and drink kumis."),
'moldovan': ("The Moldovans are Romanian-speaking people of Bessarabia, annexed by Russia in 1812 and by the Soviet Union in 1940; Moldova became independent in 1991.",
             "They are wine-makers with vast underground cellars like Milestii Mici, celebrate Martisor in spring, and eat mamaliga and placinte."),
'manchu': ("The Manchus founded the Qing dynasty, which ruled China from 1644 to 1912 and doubled its size; most of them came to speak Chinese, but their banner system and customs lasted.",
           "They keep shamanic rites, the qipao dress and the Manchu-Han imperial feast came from them, and their language survives among the Xibe and in the old archives."),
'wolof': ("The Wolof kingdoms of Jolof, Kayor and Baol ruled Senegal; the Mouride Sufi brotherhood was founded among them by Amadou Bamba in the late nineteenth century.",
          "They dance to mbalax, eat thieboudienne, rice with fish, practise teranga, hospitality, and make the great pilgrimage to Touba."),
'banda_zande': ("The Zande built kingdoms in the borderlands between the Nile and the Congo; the Banda and Gbaya lived through slave raids from the north and later colonial forced labour.",
                "They are hunters and farmers who play harps and xylophones, carve wood, and the Zande are famous for their oracles and stories of witchcraft and magic."),
'lunda_chokwe': ("The Lunda empire of the Mwata Yamvo ruled much of Central Africa in the eighteenth century; the Chokwe hunters and traders rose after it in the nineteenth.",
                 "The Chokwe carve masks of Mwana Pwo, the ideal young woman, and the Lunda kings still hold their court ceremonies."),
'lur': ("The Lurs and Bakhtiari are Iranian peoples of the Zagros mountains, famous for their seasonal migrations with flocks across high passes and rivers.",
        "They dance with handkerchiefs to the sorna and dohol, weave gabbeh rugs, and keep the Lur epics and laments."),
'santal': ("The Santals are among the largest Adivasi peoples of India; they rose in the Santal Hul of 1855 against landlords and the British.",
           "They dance in lines to the tamak and tumdak drums, worship in the sacred grove, and write their language in the Ol Chiki script."),
'afro_brazilian': ("Afro-Brazilians descend from the millions of Africans brought to Brazil; the quilombo of Palmares held out for a century, and slavery ended only in 1888.",
                   "They created samba, capoeira and Candomble, and the feijoada; Salvador da Bahia, with its Carnival and its terreiros, is their cultural capital."),
'papuan': ("The Papuans of New Guinea have lived there for over forty thousand years and were among the first farmers in the world, at Kuk swamp.",
           "They speak hundreds of languages, hold sing-sing gatherings with bird-of-paradise plumes, and grow sweet potatoes and taro."),
'naga': ("The Nagas are many tribes of the hills between India and Burma, once headhunters, who fought for independence after 1947.",
         "They hold the Hornbill festival, weave shawls with tribal patterns, build morungs (dormitories), and eat smoked pork with bamboo shoots."),
'ovambo': ("The Ovambo are the largest people of Namibia, farmers of the north; they led the SWAPO fight against South African rule until independence in 1990.",
           "They pound mahangu millet, their women wear pink dresses on festive days, and their kingdoms still have kings."),
'sahrawi': ("The Sahrawis are Hassaniya-speaking people of the Western Sahara, nomads and traders; after Spain left in 1975 their land was disputed with Morocco, and many lived in camps in Algeria.",
            "They drink three glasses of tea, wear the melhfa and darraa, keep poetry and the tent life of the desert, and play the tidinit lute."),
'balinese': ("The Balinese kept their Hindu faith when the rest of Java became Muslim; the Dutch conquest ended in the puputan, mass suicides of the courts, in 1906 and 1908.",
             "They make daily offerings, dance legong and kecak, hold the day of silence, Nyepi, and their temples and subak rice terraces are famous."),
'amhara': ("The Amhara were the ruling people of the Ethiopian empire for centuries; Ethiopia defeated Italy at Adwa in 1896 and was never colonised.",
           "They keep the Orthodox faith, fasting, Timkat and Meskel, eat injera with wot, and dance the eskista with their shoulders."),
'dogon': ("The Dogon took refuge on the Bandiagara cliffs of Mali centuries ago, building villages among the older cliff dwellings of the Tellem.",
          "They hold the Sigui festival every sixty years, dance with tall masks, and their cosmology of the stars fascinated the world."),
'latvian': ("The Latvians are a Baltic people ruled by German knights for centuries; Latvia became independent in 1918 and again in 1991 after the Baltic Way.",
            "They hold the great song and dance festival, Jani at midsummer with wreaths and caraway cheese, and eat rye bread."),
'xiang': ("The Xiang of Hunan were the frontier of the Chu kingdom; in modern times Hunan gave China many soldiers and revolutionaries, including Mao Zedong.",
          "They love the hottest food in China, smoked pork and chilli fish heads, and the Xiang embroidery and Hunan opera."),
'degar': ("The Degar or Montagnards are the highland peoples of Vietnam's Central Highlands, Jarai, Rhade, Bahnar and others, who fought with the Americans in the Vietnam war.",
          "They play gong ensembles, live in longhouses, and drink rice wine from jars through long straws."),
'kalmyk': ("The Kalmyks are Oirat Mongols who migrated from Dzungaria to the Volga in the seventeenth century, the only Buddhist people of Europe; Stalin deported them in 1943.",
           "They build Buddhist temples on the steppe, drink salted tea with milk, and keep the Jangar epic."),
'mossi': ("The Mossi kingdoms of Ouagadougou and Yatenga, founded by horsemen in the fifteenth century, resisted the empires of Mali and Songhai.",
          "The Mogho Naba still holds court on Fridays; they eat to, millet porridge, and their masks and festivals mark the farming year."),
'xhosa': ("The Xhosa fought nine frontier wars against the Cape colony; the cattle-killing of 1856-57 was a catastrophe; Nelson Mandela was a Xhosa.",
          "They speak a language with click sounds, keep the initiation of young men, and their beadwork and umngqusho dish are famous."),
'catalan': ("Catalonia grew from the Carolingian Marca Hispanica; the Crown of Aragon made it a Mediterranean power, and it lost its institutions in 1714.",
            "They build human towers, castells, dance the sardana, celebrate Sant Jordi with roses and books, and eat pa amb tomaquet."),
'sinhalese': ("The Sinhalese, by tradition, came from northern India in the fifth century BC; Anuradhapura was their ancient capital, and Sri Lanka was a centre of Theravada Buddhism.",
              "They celebrate Vesak with lanterns, the Kandy Perahera with elephants, and eat rice and curry."),
'apache_oodham': ("The Apache resisted Spain, Mexico and the United States for centuries under leaders like Cochise and Geronimo; the O'odham are desert farmers of the Sonoran desert.",
                  "They hold the Apache sunrise ceremony for girls, the crown dance, and the O'odham harvest saguaro fruit for wine."),
'haudenosaunee': ("The Haudenosaunee, the Six Nations, formed the Great Law of Peace centuries ago, a confederacy that some say inspired the US constitution.",
                  "They play lacrosse, which they invented, keep longhouse ceremonies, the clan mothers who choose chiefs, and the wampum belts."),
'yucatec': ("The Yucatec Maya built Chichen Itza, Uxmal and Mayapan; they rose in the Caste War of 1847, one of the longest indigenous revolts in the Americas.",
            "They speak Maya, cook cochinita pibil in earth ovens, live in thatched houses, and dance the jarana in white huipiles."),
'bosniak': ("The Bosniaks are South Slav Muslims of Bosnia, converted under the Ottomans; they survived the war of 1992-95 and the Srebrenica genocide.",
            "They eat cevapi, burek and baklava, sing sevdalinka love songs, and drink coffee from the dzezva in the old bazaars of Sarajevo."),
'cherokee': ("The Cherokee and Muscogee nations of the south-east built towns and constitutions; they were forced west along the Trail of Tears in the 1830s.",
             "Sequoyah created the Cherokee syllabary in 1821, and they keep stomp dances, the stickball game, and green corn ceremonies."),
'sami': ("The Sami are the indigenous people of the northern Nordic countries and the Kola peninsula; they were pressured by the states to give up their language and land.",
         "They herd reindeer, sing the yoik, wear the colourful gakti, and hold the Easter festivals of Kautokeino."),
'acholi': ("The Acholi and Lango are Nilotic peoples of northern Uganda, who suffered greatly in the war of the Lord's Resistance Army.",
           "They dance the bwola, a royal dance, and larakaraka, the courtship dance, and their reconciliation ceremony mato oput has healed wartime wounds."),
'bhil': ("The Bhils are one of India's largest tribal peoples, archers of the Aravalli and Vindhya hills who served Rajput kings and resisted British rule.",
         "They paint vivid dot pictures, celebrate Bhagoria with swings and courtship, and worship ancestral spirits and local gods."),
'visayan': ("The Visayans of the central Philippines met Magellan at Cebu in 1521, where Lapulapu killed him at Mactan, the first victory against Europe in Asia.",
            "They celebrate the Sinulog and Ati-Atihan festivals for the Santo Nino, roast lechon, and speak Cebuano and Hiligaynon."),
'kiche': ("The K'iche' Maya had a kingdom in the Guatemalan highlands; their book the Popol Vuh tells of the creation of the world and the hero twins.",
          "They weave huipiles that show their village, keep the 260-day calendar with daykeepers, and hold market days at Chichicastenango."),
'mennonite': ("The Chaco Mennonites are German-speaking Anabaptists who came to the Paraguayan Chaco from Canada and Russia in the 1920s and 1930s and turned the dry bush into farms.",
              "They speak Plautdietsch, run cooperatives and dairies, and keep a simple faith and close communities."),
'saraiki': ("The Saraiki of the south Punjab are heirs of Multan, the city of saints, one of the oldest cities of the subcontinent, and of the Bahawalpur state.",
            "They revere the Sufi poet Khwaja Ghulam Farid, dance the jhumar, wear Ajrak, and grow cotton and mangoes along the Indus."),
'yanomami': ("The Yanomami of the Guiana highlands lived in isolation until the twentieth century, when gold miners invaded their land and brought disease and violence.",
             "They live in round communal houses, the shabono, hunt and garden, and their shamans speak with the xapiri spirits."),
'bhojpuri': ("The Bhojpuris of Bihar and eastern Uttar Pradesh are heirs of ancient Magadha; many emigrated as indentured labourers to the Caribbean, Mauritius and Fiji.",
             "They celebrate Chhath with offerings to the sun at rivers, eat litti chokha, and love Bhojpuri folk songs and films."),
'songhai': ("The Songhai empire ruled the Niger bend from Gao in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries under Sonni Ali and Askia Muhammad, before falling to Morocco in 1591.",
            "They fish and farm the Niger, and the manuscripts of Timbuktu, saved from destruction in 2012, are their pride."),
'austral': ("The Austral settlers came to the ice-free coasts of Antarctica in the twenty-second century, under the Antarctic Trust, from every continent.",
            "They live in sheltered towns among the new tundra, keep midwinter festivals, and care fiercely for the fragile land they inherited."),
'tzotzil': ("The Tzotzil and Tzeltal Maya of the Chiapas highlands rose in rebellions against colonial rule and in the Zapatista rising of 1994.",
            "In San Juan Chamula they worship in a church filled with pine needles and candles; they weave and hold carnival with Maya and Catholic rites."),
'afro_carib': ("The Lesser Antilleans and Bahamians descend from Africans brought to the sugar islands; they won emancipation in 1834-38 and independence in the 1960s-80s.",
               "They celebrate Carnival with calypso and soca, Junkanoo in the Bahamas, play cricket, and eat flying fish and cou-cou."),
'kanuri': ("The Kanuri ruled the Kanem-Bornu empire around Lake Chad for a thousand years, one of the longest-lived states in history.",
           "They hold durbars with horsemen, eat millet and fish from the lake, and trade across the Sahel, where their language was for centuries a lingua franca."),
'tuareg': ("The Tuareg are Berber nomads of the Sahara; they controlled the caravan routes and resisted French rule, and rose in rebellion several times.",
           "The men wear the indigo veil, they write in Tifinagh, play the imzad fiddle and desert blues guitar, and drink three glasses of tea."),
'bashkir': ("The Bashkirs are a Turkic people of the southern Urals who joined Russia in the sixteenth century and rose many times, with Salavat Yulayev in Pugachev's rebellion.",
            "They keep honey of the wild bees, kumis and horse breeding, play the kurai flute, and hold Sabantuy in summer."),
'cajun': ("The Acadians of Nova Scotia were expelled by the British in 1755; many settled in the bayous of Louisiana and became the Cajuns.",
          "They play Cajun and zydeco music with accordion and fiddle, cook gumbo, jambalaya and crawfish boils, and hold Mardi Gras runs in the countryside."),
'lao': ("The Lao kingdom of Lan Xang, the land of a million elephants, was founded in 1353; later it was divided among Siam and France.",
        "They eat sticky rice with laap, hold the Boun Bang Fai rocket festival, play the khaen mouth organ, and give alms to monks at dawn in Luang Prabang."),
'mizo_chin': ("The Mizo and Chin live in the hills between India and Burma; British missionaries made them largely Christian, and the Mizo National Front fought for independence in the 1960s.",
              "They dance cheraw between clapping bamboo poles, weave puan cloth, and sing hymns in great choirs."),
'munduruku': ("The Munduruku of the Tapajos river were feared warriors who once ruled a large part of the Amazon and kept the heads of enemies as trophies.",
              "Today they defend their river against dams and illegal gold mines, keep their festivals, and their traditional knowledge of the forest."),
'jewish': ("The Jews are an ancient people of the land of Israel; after two thousand years of diaspora and the Holocaust, they founded the state of Israel in 1948.",
           "They keep Shabbat, Passover and Yom Kippur, speak Hebrew revived as a living language, and blend cooking from every diaspora."),
'buryat': ("The Buryats are a Mongol people around Lake Baikal, absorbed by Russia in the seventeenth century, who became Buddhists while keeping shamanism.",
           "They celebrate Sagaalgan, eat buuza dumplings, revere Baikal as sacred, and their datsan monasteries were rebuilt after Soviet times."),
'fijian': ("The Fijians are Melanesian and Polynesian; Fiji was united under Cakobau and ceded to Britain in 1874, which brought Indian indentured labourers.",
           "They drink yaqona, kava, in ceremonies, play rugby with passion, and live in villages with chiefs, the bure and the lovo earth oven."),
'kikuyu': ("The Kikuyu farmed the highlands around Mount Kenya; their land was taken by settlers, and the Mau Mau rising of the 1950s led to Kenya's independence in 1963.",
           "They are farmers and traders, eat githeri and irio, and the circumcision and age-set ceremonies still mark their lives."),
'newar': ("The Newars built the cities of the Kathmandu valley under the Malla kings and gave Nepal its art and architecture; the Gorkhas conquered them in 1768.",
          "They keep the living goddess Kumari, the Indra Jatra and chariot festivals, and their feasts, samay baji and yomari, are famous."),
'sicilian': ("Sicily was Greek, Carthaginian, Roman, Arab, Norman and Spanish before it joined Italy in 1860; every conqueror left something.",
             "They eat arancini, cannoli and pasta alla Norma, keep the puppet theatre opera dei pupi, and hold the great saints' processions of Palermo and Catania."),
'yao_mien': ("The Yao or Mien are mountain people of southern China who migrated to Vietnam, Laos and Thailand; they practise a Taoist religion with their own scriptures.",
             "They wear embroidered clothes and red turbans, hold coming-of-age ceremonies, and keep Taoist rites in Chinese characters."),
'belarusian': ("The Belarusians descend from the East Slavs of Polotsk; they lived in the Grand Duchy of Lithuania, then under Russia; the Second World War killed a quarter of them.",
               "They eat draniki potato pancakes, keep Kupalle at midsummer, weave straw crafts and sing folk songs of the forest."),
'copt': ("The Copts are the Christians of Egypt, heirs of the ancient Egyptians; their church was founded, by tradition, by Saint Mark, and monasticism began in their deserts.",
         "They keep long fasts, the desert monasteries, and the Coptic liturgy keeps the last form of the Egyptian language."),
'ewe': ("The Ewe migrated from Notsie to the coast of Togo and Ghana in the seventeenth century, and their land was divided by colonial borders between Britain and Germany.",
        "They weave their own kente, drum and dance agbadza, celebrate Hogbetsotso in memory of their escape, and keep Vodun."),
'georgian': ("Georgia became Christian in the fourth century; under Queen Tamar it had a golden age, and it lived under Persia, Ottomans and Russia before independence in 1991.",
             "They feast at the supra with a toastmaster, polyphonic songs, khachapuri, khinkali and wine fermented in clay qvevri."),
'pa_dutch': ("The Pennsylvania Dutch are descendants of German-speaking Anabaptists and others who settled Pennsylvania from 1683; the Amish and Mennonites among them kept a plain way of life.",
             "They farm with horses, build barns together, make quilts and shoofly pie, and speak Pennsylvania German."),
'igbo': ("The Igbo lived in self-governing villages with a famous tradition of enterprise; the Nri kingdom, the Biafran war of 1967-70, and the novels of Chinua Achebe mark their history.",
         "They celebrate the New Yam festival, masquerades, ofe onugbu soup, and are known as traders across Nigeria."),
'basotho': ("The Basotho were united by Moshoeshoe in the mountains of Lesotho in the nineteenth century, who defended them against the Boers and placed them under British protection.",
            "They wear blankets and the conical mokorotlo hat, ride Basotho ponies in the high mountains, and sing famo songs."),
'druze': ("The Druze faith arose in eleventh-century Egypt; the Druze live in the mountains of Lebanon, Syria and Israel, and played a great role in Lebanese history.",
          "Their faith is secret, they keep strong solidarity and hospitality, and they are known for their cooking and their white headgear."),
'garifuna': ("The Garifuna descend from Africans and Island Caribs of St Vincent, deported by the British to Central America in 1797; the Miskito of the coast were allies of Britain.",
             "They dance punta, keep their language and drums, and the Dugu ancestor ceremony; their culture is a UNESCO masterpiece."),
'igorot': ("The Igorot of the Cordillera of Luzon were never conquered by Spain; their ancestors carved the rice terraces of Banaue and the Ifugao two thousand years ago.",
           "They hold canao feasts, weave striped cloth, bury their dead in hanging coffins in Sagada, and keep their mountain agriculture."),
'walloon': ("The Walloons are the French-speaking people of southern Belgium, heirs of the first industrial revolution on the continent, of coal and steel along the Meuse.",
            "They keep the giants processions and the Gilles of Binche at carnival, eat boulets and waffles of Liege, and brew Trappist beer."),
'baloch': ("The Baloch spread across the arid lands of the Iranian plateau and the Makran coast; their Khanate of Kalat lasted until 1955, and their land is split between Iran, Pakistan and Afghanistan.",
           "They keep a code of honour and hospitality, sajji roasted lamb, embroidered dress, and the music of the suroz fiddle."),
'kayapo_xavante': ("The Kayapo and Xavante are Ge peoples of central Brazil; the Kayapo leaders Raoni and others fought to protect their lands from dams and ranchers.",
                   "They paint their bodies in black and red geometric patterns, run log relay races, wear feather headdresses, and hold great ceremonies of naming."),
'mbundu': ("The Mbundu kingdom of Ndongo resisted the Portuguese for decades under Queen Nzinga in the seventeenth century, one of the greatest leaders of African history.",
           "Kimbundu is their language, Luanda lies in their land, and they created semba music, mother of kizomba, and the carnival of Luanda."),
'sukuma': ("The Sukuma and Nyamwezi are the largest peoples of Tanzania; the Nyamwezi were the great caravan traders of the interior under chiefs like Mirambo.",
           "Sukuma dance societies compete with snakes and porcupines in their acts, and their villages farm cotton and herd cattle south of Lake Victoria."),
'afro_colombian': ("Afro-Colombians descend from Africans enslaved in the gold mines and plantations; Palenque de San Basilio was the first free town of escaped slaves in the Americas.",
                   "They play the marimba de chonta of the Pacific coast, dance currulao and champeta, and speak Palenquero, a Spanish-based creole."),
'finnic': ("The Finns lived under Swedish rule for six centuries and as a Russian grand duchy before independence in 1917; they defended it in the Winter War of 1939-40.",
           "They live by the sauna, sisu (grit), the Kalevala epic, berries and mushrooms of the forest, rye bread, and the lake cottage in summer."),
'lithuanian': ("The Grand Duchy of Lithuania was the last pagan state of Europe and one of its largest; it united with Poland, was absorbed by Russia, and was freed from the Soviets in 1990.",
               "They sing sutartines in rounds, keep Jonines at midsummer, eat cepelinai, and play basketball as their second religion."),
'miao': ("The Miao and Hmong are mountain people of southern China; many rebelled against the Qing, and some migrated to Laos, Vietnam and Thailand, fighting in the Indochina wars.",
         "They are famous for silver headdresses, batik and embroidery, the New Year with courtship games, and the reed pipe lusheng."),
'taiwanese_ind': ("The Taiwanese Austronesians are the original peoples of Taiwan, Amis, Atayal, Paiwan and others, from whom the Austronesian expansion across the Pacific may have begun.",
                  "They hold harvest festivals with song and dance, weave, tattoo, and their languages are among the most diverse of the family."),
'armenian': ("Armenia became the first Christian nation in 301, invented its alphabet in 405, and lived under many empires; the genocide of 1915 killed a million and scattered the survivors worldwide.",
             "They keep the Armenian Apostolic Church, the duduk, lavash, khorovats, apricots, and a diaspora of millions."),
'crimean_tatar': ("The Crimean Tatars ruled the Crimean Khanate under the Giray dynasty until Russia annexed it in 1783; Stalin deported the whole people to Central Asia in 1944, and they returned only after 1989.",
                  "They eat chiburek and yantyk, sing and dance the haytarma, and remember the deportation every 18 May."),
'jamaican': ("Jamaica was a British sugar colony where enslaved Africans outnumbered whites; the Maroons fought the British to a treaty, and Jamaica became independent in 1962.",
             "They gave the world reggae, ska and dancehall, Bob Marley, Rastafari, jerk chicken, ackee and saltfish, and sprinters like Usain Bolt."),
'khmer': ("The Khmer empire ruled from Angkor, the largest city of the medieval world, between the ninth and fifteenth centuries; modern Cambodia lived through the Khmer Rouge genocide of the 1970s.",
          "They keep apsara dance, Theravada Buddhism, amok fish curry, prahok, and the water festival on the Tonle Sap."),
'ndyuka': ("The Ndyuka and Saramaka are Maroons who escaped from the plantations of Suriname and signed treaties with the Dutch in the 1760s, keeping their freedom in the forest.",
           "They carve beautiful wooden paddles and combs, speak English-based creoles, keep African-derived religions and paint their houses."),
'ugric': ("The Khanty, Mansi and Nenets are Uralic peoples of western Siberia; they hunt, fish and herd reindeer, and were drawn into the oil and gas boom of the twentieth century.",
          "They hold the bear festival, keep sacred places in the forest, and the Nenets migrate with their herds across the Yamal peninsula."),
'zhuang': ("The Zhuang are the largest minority of China, a Tai people of Guangxi who farmed rice on terraces long before Han settlers arrived; their leaders resisted the empire many times.",
           "They hold singing festivals where young people meet by trading improvised songs, play the bronze drums, and weave brocade."),
}
